<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Æthelberht II of East Anglia</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 794</span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a> · Also known as Saint Ethelbert the King</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Æthelberht II was king of East Anglia in the late 8th century, known from a few coins and later accounts. In 794 he was beheaded on the orders of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/offa-of-mercia">Offa of Mercia</a>, according to tradition after coming to Offa's court to seek the hand of his daughter. He was venerated as a saint and martyr, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hereford">Hereford</a> Cathedral is dedicated to him.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/aethelberht-ii-of-east-anglia.jpg" alt="Æthelberht II of East Anglia" width="320" height="445" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Anonymous; incorporated into book by illustrator George Cowley Haddon (c.1839-1885). · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:St_Ethelbert_the_King.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Few East Anglian records survive from this period, and medieval writers filled the gap with accounts of their own. The earliest extended one, the Passio S. Æthelberhti, written around 1080 and tied to his cult at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hereford">Hereford</a>, names his parents as Æthelred I of East Anglia and Leofrana of Mercia and tells of his piety, his election as king and his wise rule. Osbert of Clare and Gerald of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a> retold it, and William of Malmesbury, Roger of Wendover and Matthew Paris borrowed from it. A much later saint's life gives 779 as the start of his reign, on uncertain authority.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>His coins are among the few contemporary sources for him. Four pennies were known by 2014, one found that March in a Sussex field. A penny said to have been found at Tivoli, near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, in 1908 bears the title REX and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romulus">Romulus</a> and Remus with the she-wolf, and names his moneyer Lul, who also struck coins for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/offa-of-mercia">Offa</a>.</p><p>The numismatist Marion Archibald reads such coins as a sub-king claiming more independence than <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/offa-of-mercia">Offa</a> would tolerate, and Rory Naismith suggests they were issued either during a spell of East Anglian independence or under an arrangement to share minting rights. The she-wolf may also have claimed descent from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wuffingas">Wuffingas</a>, at a time when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Viking</a> raids on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lindisfarne">Lindisfarne</a> in 793 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jarrow">Jarrow</a> a year later called for strong kingship.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxon-chronicle">Anglo-Saxon Chronicle</a> records only that he was beheaded on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/offa-of-mercia">Offa</a>'s orders, at Sutton in Herefordshire according to Andy Todd or at nearby Marden according to Michael Lapidge. Later writers made Offa's queen, Cynethryth, ever more central: John of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/worcester">Worcester</a> has her persuade Offa, and Roger of Wendover a trap in which the king falls through his bedroom floor to her executioners. Such a killing of one <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxons">Anglo-Saxon</a> king on another's orders was very rare. He was locally canonised, his feast falls on 20 May, and he shares the patronage of Hereford Cathedral with the Virgin Mary; eleven of his church dedications are in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norfolk">Norfolk</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suffolk">Suffolk</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Æthelberht II of East Anglia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Offa (757–796) went further, annexing Kent and Sussex, having <b>Æthelberht II of East Anglia</b> beheaded in 794 and persuading the papacy to create an archbishopric at Lichfield in 787, which lasted until 803.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-mercia">Kingdom of Mercia · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-east-anglia">Kingdom of East Anglia</a>. Died in Herefordshire, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=52.08,-2.75&amp;year=784" aria-label="Herefordshire on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4488 364 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Æthelberht II of East Anglia"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4938 634h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4938 634h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=52.08,-2.75&amp;year=784">What was here in 784 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
